A wrong grid rejects the entire submitted hypothesis, but binary feedback does not locate the error. Do not respond by shifting the same speculative recoloring boundary or trying arbitrary minor variants. First compare every demo input/output cellwise and list the precise changed coordinates, including isolated endpoint changes and preserved lines. Distinguish object-internal changes from continuation/tail changes, and test predictions against all examples.

Request another demo only when you can state which live candidate rules it could separate. Otherwise derive a materially different rule from the examples and apply it once, preserving all cells not selected by that rule. This discipline prevents a sequence of unsupported guesses from spending the attempt budget.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/failed-hypothesis-revision.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/this-session-lessons.md", "content": "# Costly Continual-ARC action discipline

This session illustrates two recurring hazards in sparse-object transformations. First, a visually plausible operation on the test grid is not evidence: the initial submission added a color to the top row even though no demo supported that change. Second, after a rejection, a shifted copy of the motif is still a weak cosmetic variant unless a rule derived from the demonstrations predicts it.

Before acting, describe the mapping of all changed and unchanged demo cells and apply the candidate transformation to each full demo. In this case the demos are not enough to justify either the top-row insertion or simply translating the test motif upward; the second demo's input and output differ only by a one-row downward translation of the object, while the first pair has a particular top-row modification unrelated to a generic translation. Treat that discrepancy as a concrete unresolved rule question. Spend another demo request when it can discriminate between a context-triggered edit and object/position-based transformation, rather than guessing or making a cosmetic revision. After rejection, rebuild the hypothesis from the entire evidence set before spending another submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-checkpoint/references/this-session-lessons.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/instance-learning-panel-rule.md", "content": "# Learning panel-label rules from compressed outputs

When a separator-delimited grid shrinks to one output cell per panel, the lattice and ordering are only the first inference step. A plausible grid with matching dimensions can still be wrong because the cell-label semantics are underdetermined.

## Panel evidence ledger

For every demonstration, align each panel with its output coordinate and record:
- marker/background/separator colors;
- all exceptional-cell coordinates, counts, and arrangement;
- whether features align across panel rows or columns;
- the output value for that panel.

Normalize palettes across examples. Test panel-local rules (presence, count, shape, location) and cross-panel rules (aligned feature sequences, repeated rows/columns, or spatial configurations) against every cell of every output. Distinguish selecting a panel from assigning its output label; the output can encode a relationship rather than copy the local marker color.

## Costly action checkpoint

After each demo, update the ledger before requesting another. Name the competing rules and whether they produce different predictions on the active test. After an incorrect submission, preserve only validated structural facts (such as panel dimensions/order), discard the rejected semantic mapping, and rebuild from all demonstrations. Binary feedback identifies no particular cell, so do not make arbitrary local edits. Spend another demo only when it can discriminate live rules enough to justify its fixed cost.

Always preserve the strict one-action JSON protocol when the evaluator requires it.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/instance-learning-panel-rule.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/failed-noisy-grid-inference.md", "content": "# Failed noisy-grid inference: preserve full changed-cell evidence

This attempt stream involved heterogeneous noisy-background demonstrations and a compact third-color motif. Multiple unsupported test candidates were submitted: recoloring the marker-adjacent cells, 4-/8-connected zero components, enclosed zero cells, and finally leaving the grid unchanged. All were rejected. The demos had visibly different changed masks and did not establish a simple uniform flood-fill rule; connectivity and proximity alone were not sufficient evidence.

Reusable lesson: compare every demo's changed and preserved cells before selecting a transformation. Explicitly test competing neighborhood/connectivity rules across complete pairs and inspect exceptions; do not infer global propagation merely from a marker in one example. After binary rejection, discard the whole candidate, not just a guessed boundary. Once the demo cap is exhausted, avoid cycling through local variants or reverting to input unchanged without evidence. This note records failed candidates, not a validated alternative rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/failed-noisy-grid-inference.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/stripe-sequence-validation.md", "content": "# Uniform stripe sequence tasks: validation and revision

For grids made of full-width, single-color rows, collapse each grid to a 1-D sequence of row colors before inferring the transformation. Compare input and output sequences position by position, including leading and trailing background rows and every gap between visible stripes. Check whether output preserves anchor rows, repeats a motif, extends a sequence, or uses a phase determined by the first/last marker. Do not infer a 2-D periodic tiling merely because other tasks in the broader benchmark use diagonal traces; select the transformation family supported by the current demos.

After a wrong answer, binary feedback rejects the whole candidate but gives no localization. Re-evaluate the rule itself against all demos rather than making repeated near-duplicate grids. Before serializing, validate the output dimensions, count every row's width, and ensure all cell values are valid integers 0–9.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/stripe-sequence-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/unchanged-demo-and-failed-hypothesis.md", "content": "# Unchanged demos and hypothesis revision

An input-output pair with no changes is an active constraint, not a non-example. A salient sparse motif may be inert or lack the feature that triggers the demonstrated operation. Compare every demo for changed and preserved coordinates, including clusters that look similar but receive different treatment.

After a wrong submission, reject the complete hypothesis and revisit the transformation family; don't make cosmetic coordinate edits. Separate trigger, propagation geometry, selected extent, and handling of original markers. A single demo that extends a diagonal may support a ray hypothesis, but unchanged sparse clusters elsewhere are counterevidence to blindly extending every similar-looking track. Request more only when an example can distinguish specific alternatives; otherwise form a materially revised candidate from the full evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/unchanged-demo-and-failed-hypothesis.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/failed-global-fill-rejection.md", "content": "# Rejected global-fill hypothesis: preserve geometry, audit exact changes

In this Continual-ARC instance, several demos used noisy grids with one distinctive-color object. An attempted submission globally flood-filled every zero connected to the marker color, and binary feedback rejected it. The failure illustrates why connectivity alone is not sufficient evidence for recoloring: the demos preserve many background cells, and different examples show selective additions localized to patterned regions rather than blanket access from a seed.

After rejection, do not make a small arbitrary edit or repeat the same flood-fill with a different boundary. Reconstruct exact changed coordinates for every input/output pair, identify the marker object's shape and candidate repeated/translated structures, and check every unchanged background region. If the demos still support incompatible rules, request another only with a stated discriminating question; otherwise use a materially different candidate and validate it against the full evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/failed-global-fill-rejection.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/noisy-background-pattern-propagation.md", "content": "# Noisy-background pattern propagation checkpoint

When a small colored motif is embedded in a mostly two-color noisy field and outputs add that motif color at many scattered locations, do not infer a rule from visual resemblance alone. Compare every input-output pair cell by cell and form the exact changed-cell mask. Test exact motif placements, orientations, overlaps, and near-matches; conspicuous uncolored near-matches are negative evidence that a candidate selection rule must explain. Check that original marker cells are preserved and that all additions—not merely many of them—follow one consistent criterion. Before requesting another demo, state at least two surviving rules, their distinct predictions for the active test, and the concrete example outcome that would discriminate them. A further noisy grid without such a discriminator is not an evidence-led request.

This is a general audit method, not a claim that motif-copying is the right explanation for every noisy grid. See also `noisy-region-hypothesis-audit.md` and `overlapping-pattern-copy-audit.md` for connectivity and false-positive placement pitfalls.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-checkpoint/references/noisy-background-pattern-propagation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/noisy-motif-review.md", "content": "# Noisy motif transformation review

For a sparse colored motif embedded in a noisy binary field, record the exact changed-cell mask for every demonstration before proposing a rule. Distinguish seed motif cells, random background coincidences, and newly transformed cells. Check all apparent translated motif placements and treat untransformed near-matches as negative evidence; do not infer template-copying merely because some scattered locations resemble the seed. Compare complete masks across demos, including preserved cells and dimensions.

After an incorrect submission, discard the complete mechanism rather than adjusting only a palette mapping, phase, or a few cells. Rebuild the candidate set from all demonstrations and choose only a materially distinct rule whose trigger, extent, and exceptions explain every observed change. If evidence remains underdetermined, be candid internally about that uncertainty and spend the remaining attempt on the most demo-consistent whole-grid prediction, not a sequence of cosmetic variants.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-checkpoint/references/noisy-motif-review.md"}
